<h2 id="when" style="color:#1565c0;font-size:1.22em;font-weight:650;border-left:4px solid #42a5f5;padding-left:10px;margin-top:1.1em">1. When this applies</h2>

| Item | Behavior |
|------|----------|
| **Input** | Path to a `.md` (user-named, attached, or clearly implied). If ambiguous, ask once. |
| **Output** | Same basename as the `.md`, sibling `.pdf` unless the user names another path. |
| **Mermaid** | Diagrams **must** render in the PDF. Never ship a PDF that shows fenced Mermaid as monospace source. |
| **Do not** | Commit the PDF unless the user asks. Do not leave large temp dirs behind. |

---

<h2 id="prereqs" style="color:#1565c0;font-size:1.22em;font-weight:650;border-left:4px solid #42a5f5;padding-left:10px;margin-top:1.1em">2. Prerequisites</h2>

Check once per session; install only what is missing:

| Tool | Check | Install (if needed) |
|------|--------|---------------------|
| **Mermaid CLI** | `mmdc --version` | `npm install -g @mermaid-js/mermaid-cli` |
| **pandoc** | `pandoc --version` | OS package / https://pandoc.org |
| **Chrome** (or Chromium) | macOS: `/Applications/Google Chrome.app/.../Google Chrome` | Install Chrome if headless print is used |

**Required for diagrams:** `mmdc`. Prefer the [Preferred pipeline](#preferred). Use [Fallback](#fallback) only if `mmdc` cannot run.

---

<h2 id="preferred" style="color:#1565c0;font-size:1.22em;font-weight:650;border-left:4px solid #42a5f5;padding-left:10px;margin-top:1.1em">3. Preferred pipeline (`mmdc` → images → PDF)</h2>

Work in a **temp directory** next to the source (e.g. `.mdpdf_tmp/` under the same folder as the `.md`). Do **not** permanently rewrite the user’s source `.md` unless they ask.

### 3.1 Extract and render Mermaid

1. Find every fenced block: opening fence language **`mermaid`**.
2. For each block `i = 1..N`, write body to `diagram_i.mmd`.
3. Render PNG (or SVG):

```bash
mmdc -i diagram_i.mmd -o diagram_i.png -b transparent
```

4. Build a **temp** Markdown copy: replace each ` ```mermaid ... ``` ` with:

```markdown
![Diagram i](diagram_i.png)
```

Use paths relative to the temp Markdown file so pandoc/Chrome resolve images.

### 3.2 Emit PDF

**Option A — Chrome print (best for HTML headings / styled tables):**

1. `pandoc temp.md -f markdown+raw_html -t html5 -o temp.html --standalone --resource-path=.:<tmp_dir>`
2. Wrap or ensure basic print CSS (A4 margins, readable font, `page-break-inside: avoid` on images).
3. Headless print (adjust Chrome path for the OS):

```bash
"/Applications/Google Chrome.app/Contents/MacOS/Google Chrome" \
  --headless=new --disable-gpu --no-pdf-header-footer \
  --virtual-time-budget=5000 \
  --print-to-pdf="/absolute/path/out.pdf" \
  "file:///absolute/path/temp.html"
```

**Option B — pandoc engine (simpler text; weaker HTML styling):**

```bash
pandoc temp.md -o out.pdf --pdf-engine=xelatex -V geometry:margin=2cm
```

If `xelatex` fails, try `pdflatex` or fall back to Option A.

### 3.3 Verify

- Confirm PDF exists and size is non-trivial (diagrams usually push size well above a text-only PDF).
- Prefer `pdfinfo` / page count when available; open-path report to the user.

---

<h2 id="fallback" style="color:#1565c0;font-size:1.22em;font-weight:650;border-left:4px solid #42a5f5;padding-left:10px;margin-top:1.1em">4. Fallback pipeline (no `mmdc`)</h2>

Use only if Mermaid CLI is unavailable:

1. Convert Markdown → HTML with pandoc (`markdown+raw_html`).
2. Replace each Mermaid fence with `<div class="mermaid">…</div>`.
3. Inject Mermaid from CDN (`mermaid@10`+) and `await mermaid.run()`.
4. Chrome headless `--print-to-pdf` with enough `--virtual-time-budget` (e.g. 10000–15000) so diagrams finish before print.
5. Needs network for the CDN unless Mermaid is vendored locally.

Prefer installing `mmdc` over relying on this path long-term.

---

<h2 id="output" style="color:#1565c0;font-size:1.22em;font-weight:650;border-left:4px solid #42a5f5;padding-left:10px;margin-top:1.1em">5. Output and cleanup</h2>

1. Write the final PDF next to the source `.md` (same stem) unless the user specifies another path.
2. Delete the temp dir (`.mmd` / `.png` / intermediate `.html`) after a successful PDF.
3. Reply with the **absolute path**, page count if known, and note that Mermaid was rendered via **`mmdc`** (or fallback).

---

<h2 id="checklist" style="color:#1565c0;font-size:1.22em;font-weight:650;border-left:4px solid #42a5f5;padding-left:10px;margin-top:1.1em">6. Self-check</h2>

- [ ] Correct input `.md` identified.
- [ ] `mmdc` available (or fallback documented).
- [ ] Every Mermaid fence rendered as an image (or CDN SVG) in the PDF.
- [ ] Source `.md` left unchanged unless the user asked to rewrite it.
- [ ] Temp artifacts removed; PDF path reported to the user.
